Each s += x copies the ENTIRE accumulated string into a fresh allocation, then appends. Ten appends copy 1+2+3+...+10 = 55 strings' worth — quadratic.

```go
// BAD: O(n²)
var s string
for i := 0; i < 1000; i++ {
    s += "chunk"
}

// GOOD: strings.Builder
var b strings.Builder
for i := 0; i < 1000; i++ {
    b.WriteString("chunk")
}
s := b.String()   // executed: one buffer, one final copy
```

Builder is a mutable byte slice internally (append's grow-by-double) with String() doing ONE copy at the end.

The family: strings.Builder for joining text; bytes.Buffer when you need bytes anyway; strings.Join for 'join with separator' (computes final size up front — zero realloc); fmt.Sprintf when formatting matters more than allocation (~3-5x slower — fine for UI, wrong in hot loops).

The detection test: any += or s = s + in a loop is O(n²) — flag it. The rewrite is always one of the four.
